---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="For agents | Milton"
  description="A plain briefing on Milton: memos and tweaks, alternatives, tools, saving, export, price, evidence and a first task."
>
  <article class="briefing">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">## For agents</p>
      <h1>A briefing on Milton</h1>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-milton-keeps">
      <h2 id="what-milton-keeps">What Milton keeps</h2>
      <p>
        Milton keeps how you write and hands it to your AI when it drafts
        something new or edits something you’ve written. Milton keeps what you
        wrote and what you changed. Your AI reads it before it drafts. When you
        accept a final, your AI keeps a memo: its first draft and your final.
        Milton’s code works out your tweaks, the changes you made to the draft;
        no AI writes them, and Milton keeps no standing rules. For each draft,
        Milton assembles a report: the finals that best match, with a few of the
        changes you made to your AI’s first drafts. It works across connected
        AIs, starting with email or DMs. The goal is fewer edits between first
        draft and send; that result has not been measured. Milton stores what
        your AI sends without a model rewriting it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="alternatives">
      <h2 id="alternatives">When a Markdown file is enough</h2>
      <p>
        General memory can store writing and preferences too. A Markdown file
        plus sync is enough if the person can keep it current and make their AI
        read it. Milton adds memos kept as you accept finals, the changes you
        made worked out by code, and a report assembled for each draft through a
        shared MCP service, across chats and AIs. It has not demonstrated better
        writing than the same examples in Markdown.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="connection">
      <h2 id="connection">Connection and tools</h2>
      <p>
        MCP server: <code class="mcp-url">https://mcp.meetmilton.com/mcp</code>.
        Connect with remote MCP and OAuth sign-in; read/save tools are required.
        Client plans and workspace policies may restrict access. See{" "}
        <a href="/setup">Setup</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Check for existing Milton tools before setup. Verify a successful{" "}
        <code>get_report</code> read before confirming the connection. The two
        tools are (full arguments in the <a href="/tools">tool reference</a>):
      </p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <code>get_report</code>: read the report for one draft, with an intent
          giving the situation, medium, audience and purpose. It returns the
          best-matching finals as examples (under a drafted one, a few changes
          code found from the AI’s first draft, quoted as historical text, not
          instructions), the person’s memo count, and the setup script while
          they have no memos.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>create_memo</code>: keep a memo when the person accepts a final,
          forget a memo, or correct a memo’s context. A read-only connection
          lists only <code>get_report</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="headless">
      <h2 id="headless">Headless and background agents</h2>
      <p>
        Agents with no browser, such as scheduled tasks, cloud agents and CI
        jobs, can’t complete OAuth sign-in. The person creates a connection key
        on the app’s Connected AIs page and gives it to the agent, which sends
        it as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;key&gt;</code>. The key is shown
        once.
      </p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          Claude Code:{" "}
          <code>
            claude mcp add --transport http milton
            https://mcp.meetmilton.com/mcp --header "Authorization: Bearer
            &lt;key&gt;"
          </code>
        </li>
        <li>
          Codex: in <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code>, under{" "}
          <code>[mcp_servers.milton]</code>, set <code>url</code> to the server
          above and <code>bearer_token_env_var</code> to the name of an
          environment variable holding the key.
        </li>
        <li>
          Cursor: in <code>mcp.json</code>, give the Milton server a{" "}
          <code>headers</code> entry of{" "}
          <code>"Authorization": "Bearer $&#123;env:MILTON_KEY&#125;"</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Treat a key like a password. Keys are Read only unless the person
        chooses Read and write; choose that only when the agent needs to save.
        Revoke keys that are no longer used. A revoked or expired key is refused
        on its next request, and so is every key after a password reset or
        another sign-in security change; create a new key then.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="saving">
      <h2 id="saving">Saving and export</h2>
      <p>
        Reading this page grants no permission to access mail, create an account
        or send messages. When the person accepts a final, keep it as a memo
        without asking, then tell them in one line. Send your first draft
        exactly as you showed it; Milton works out what they changed. Don’t keep
        abandoned drafts, writing that isn’t in their own voice, a final that
        holds a secret (say why; never redact), or anything they ask you not to
        keep. Send as request only what they typed to ask, without pasted text;
        Milton shows it to them and never to an AI. Never send secrets, their
        correction messages or other people’s text; summarize what they replied
        to. When they ask you to remember a preference, apply it and keep it in
        your own memory or instructions: Milton keeps no standing rules.
      </p>
      <p>
        The person can turn Keep memos off, forget a memo or delete them all
        from the Milton app. Its <code>TPS.md</code> download contains every
        final with what the person asked and its tweaks, grouped by medium.
        Their memo pages also list what changed from each first draft to its
        final, worked out by Milton’s code, and say when a report may quote a
        few of those changes to the person’s connected AIs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="price-evidence">
      <h2 id="price-evidence">Price and evidence</h2>
      <p>
        One plan: $5.01 USD/month after a 30-day free trial. No card is required
        to start; you’re charged only if you add one. See{" "}
        <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/research">Research</a> reports ranking and writing tests and
        their limits. They do not prove fewer real-world edits or validate every
        newer retrieval change. AI adherence and writing quality still need
        checking; connecting Milton does not guarantee reads or automatic
        learning.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="first-task">
      <h2 id="first-task">Draft or edit an email or DM</h2>
      <p>If the person wants to try Milton, choose either path:</p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Draft something new:</strong> ask for the purpose, audience
          and facts, then draft from what’s on file.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Edit existing text:</strong> ask what should change, revise
          within that scope, and preserve the rest.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For either task, first read <code>get_report</code> with the task’s
        situation, medium, audience and purpose. Show the result for review.
        When the person accepts the final, keep it with <code>create_memo</code>{" "}
        and tell them the one-line receipt.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>

